19:36:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really sharp observation — and you've put your finger on the most common point of confusion. You’re right that without the engines running, a typical airliner can’t stay up indefinitely. But the nuance is this: **the engines are not holding the plane up; they are preventing it from slowing down.** Let’s break it down. ### Why a plane without engines doesn’t immediately “drop out of the sky” If the engines fail at cruising altitude, the plane doesn’t fall straight down like a stone. It becomes a **glider**. The wings are still generating lift as long as the plane moves forward through the air. The plane will gradually descend, trading altitude for the speed needed to keep air flowing over the wings. A commercial jet with no engines can glide for many miles (the “Gimli Glider” famously glided to a safe landing after running out of fuel). This proves that **lift comes from the wings, not the engines**. The engines just supply the energy to overcome **drag** (air resistance) and maintain enough speed so the wings keep flying. ### So why do we say engines “keep it in the air”? Because in level, sustained flight, you need a constant forward force to counteract drag. Without it, drag slows the aircraft, which reduces lift. When lift drops below weight, the plane descends. So: - Engines → thrust → overcomes drag → maintains speed → wings maintain lift → plane stays up. In that chain, the engines are **necessary, but not sufficient** on their own. The wings are the actual lifting devices. The engine is the “energy source” that keeps the wing working. ### A helpful analogy Think of a bicycle. If you stop pedaling, you don’t instantly fall over. You coast for a while, but eventually, friction and air resistance slow you down until you’d tip over. The pedaling doesn’t hold you upright — your balance and the gyroscopic effect of the wheels do that. But without pedaling (or a downhill slope), you’ll eventually lose speed and fall. The engine i