19:37:49 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s an excellent follow-up — you’ve gone from “what lifts the plane” to “why the engine size scales with weight.” And the answer ties your hydrofoil analogy even tighter. ### Heavier plane → more lift needed To keep a heavier plane in the air, the wings must generate more lift. Lift is proportional to the wing’s area, the angle of attack, the air density, and — critically — the *square* of the airspeed. For a given wing, the only practical way to get more lift is to **fly faster**. (You can also increase the angle of attack, but only up to a point before stalling.) So a heavy airliner has to fly at a higher cruising speed than a light one, or it must have much larger wings (and large wings add weight and drag themselves). In practice, heavy jets use a combination of larger wings *and* higher speed. ### Higher speed → vastly more drag Here’s the kicker: aerodynamic drag increases with the *square* of speed. Twice the speed means four times the drag. And there’s a special kind of drag called **induced drag** that is directly related to lift — lift itself creates drag. A heavier plane generating more lift inevitably produces more induced drag. So a heavier plane flies faster to get enough lift, which ramps up **total drag** enormously. To maintain that speed against all that drag, you need **more thrust**. That’s why bigger engines. ### The engine is fighting drag, not weight Think of it like this: the engines don’t care how heavy the plane is *directly*. They care how much air resistance is pushing backward. But the weight of the plane dictates the speed you need, which dictates the drag. So weight sets the propulsion requirement indirectly. Imagine you’re pushing a box across a floor. A heavier box has more friction, so you need a bigger push. You’re not lifting the box; you’re overcoming the friction that exists *because* of its weight. Here, the engine overcomes aerodynamic drag, which exists *because* of the lift needed to support the weight. Same chain: weight → lift requirement → speed requirement → drag → thrust requirement. ### The hydrofoil analogy completes the picture You liked the hydrofoil. A heavier hydrofoil boat needs a bigger engine not because the engine lifts the boat, but because: - The hydrofoils must generate more upward force to lift the heavier hull. - To get that force, the boat must go faster (or have bigger foils). - Higher speed creates much more water drag. - So you need a bigger engine to overcome that drag. If you simply put a massive engine on a hydrofoil and kept the foils small, the boat would eventually go fast enough to lift, but the foils might be overloaded or the drag might be too high. The foils (wings) are still doing the lifting; the engine is meeting the increased energy demand. ### So why not just use bigger wings and slower speed? You could, to a point, but then you get an unwieldy, draggy wing that’s heavy itself and difficult to build. Airliners are optimized for a sweet spot where the engine size and wing size balance cost, speed, and efficiency. A heavier plane inevitably needs either bigger engines (to go faster) or huge, high-drag wings. The engineers choose the engine size that fits the overall design. Thus, the correlation you spotted — heavier plane, bigger engines — is real, but it’s because the **wings demand more speed to lift more weight**, and that speed demands more thrust to overcome the drag that comes with it. The engines never directly lift the weight; they just provide the ever‑increasing forward force that keeps the lifting cycle going.
